1
00:00:00.371 --> 00:00:03.759
Now that you've seen the high-level architecture of a RAG system,

2
00:00:03.759 --> 00:00:08.399
let's take a closer look at each component, starting with the large language model itself.

3
00:00:09.119 --> 00:00:14.399
Understanding the way they operate, their strengths, and their limitations will help explain

4
00:00:14.399 --> 00:00:19.120
the way other components in a RAG system are designed to improve an LLM's performance.

5
00:00:19.760 --> 00:00:25.520
LLMs are sometimes jokingly called fancy autocomplete, but that's actually a fair

6
00:00:25.520 --> 00:00:31.600
description. All LLMs do is predict the next word that should appear in a piece of text.

7
00:00:32.400 --> 00:00:37.680
For example, if I show you the incomplete phrase, what a beautiful day the sun is dot dot dot,

8
00:00:38.319 --> 00:00:41.520
you and I could probably guess what word should complete that sentence.

9
00:00:42.159 --> 00:00:48.240
And so could an LLM. I'd say the word shining makes the most sense, but the sun is rising,

10
00:00:48.240 --> 00:00:52.799
or the sun is out could also work. The original phrase is called the prompt,

11
00:00:52.799 --> 00:00:56.319
and each of these final completed phrases is called a completion.

12
00:00:56.959 --> 00:01:02.959
One possible completion for this prompt is the sun is exploding, but you'd probably say that's

13
00:01:02.959 --> 00:01:09.040
incorrect. The problem isn't that the phrase is grammatically flawed, it's valid English,

14
00:01:09.040 --> 00:01:15.279
but just that this phrase is improbable. The sun usually doesn't explode, certainly not on a

15
00:01:15.279 --> 00:01:21.839
beautiful day like this one. You have an intuitive sense for how words are used, and in a way,

16
00:01:21.839 --> 00:01:27.440
so do language models. Under the hood, they use a neural network, an enormous and complex

17
00:01:27.440 --> 00:01:33.599
mathematical model of language. This model stores information about which words are commonly used

18
00:01:33.599 --> 00:01:40.239
with each other, in which order they typically appear, and on a higher level, captures what

19
00:01:40.239 --> 00:01:46.800
these words mean in context. This mathematical representation of language is what the model

20
00:01:46.800 --> 00:01:53.279
ultimately uses to generate new text. When an LLM generates a completion, all it's doing is

21
00:01:53.279 --> 00:01:59.839
adding new words to the end of the prompt, one word at a time. For example, if it were generating

22
00:01:59.839 --> 00:02:05.919
a completion to that last prompt, it would likely add the word shining, and then might add the words

23
00:02:05.919 --> 00:02:12.080
in the sky one at a time, before signaling the completion is done and returning it to the user.

24
00:02:12.720 --> 00:02:19.600
Technically, an LLM doesn't generate words, but rather tokens, which is a more generic term for

25
00:02:19.600 --> 00:02:27.520
pieces of words. Some words like London and door might have their own token, but common compound

26
00:02:27.520 --> 00:02:35.279
words like programmatically and unhappy are usually split into multiple tokens. Punctuation,

27
00:02:35.279 --> 00:02:40.960
like the period, comma, and the question mark, could also get their own tokens. Most LLMs have

28
00:02:40.960 --> 00:02:48.559
a total vocabulary of anywhere from 10 to more than 100,000 tokens. Having the flexibility to

29
00:02:48.559 --> 00:02:55.440
build up compound words out of smaller word pieces allows the model to build any possible word

30
00:02:55.440 --> 00:03:01.839
without needing to actually assign a token to each one. Here's the process the LLM goes through

31
00:03:01.839 --> 00:03:07.919
before adding each new token to the completion. First, it processes the current state of the

32
00:03:07.919 --> 00:03:14.399
completion, generating a deep understanding of the relationships between each word and the overall

33
00:03:14.399 --> 00:03:20.559
meaning of the text. Then, the LLM goes through every token in its vocabulary, usually tens to

34
00:03:20.559 --> 00:03:25.440
hundreds of thousands of them, and calculates the probability that it should appear next.

35
00:03:26.080 --> 00:03:30.559
In the example you've seen so far, shining would likely have the highest probability,

36
00:03:30.559 --> 00:03:36.800
and rising would have a smaller probability. But even improbable words like exploding or snoring

37
00:03:36.800 --> 00:03:43.759
would still have a tiny chance of appearing next. In other words, the LLM generates a probability

38
00:03:43.759 --> 00:03:52.000
distribution across every token. It then randomly chooses the next token from that probability

39
00:03:52.000 --> 00:04:00.479
distribution. In this example, 80 times out of 100, it'll choose shining. But it's still possible it

40
00:04:00.479 --> 00:04:07.919
could choose rising, or yes, even exploding. When the LLM goes to add another token to the completion,

41
00:04:07.919 --> 00:04:13.679
it repeats this entire process. This time, most of the words in the completion are from the original

42
00:04:13.679 --> 00:04:20.720
prompt, but one of them is the word shining, which it just generated. This means that token choices

43
00:04:20.720 --> 00:04:27.359
the LLM makes earlier in the completion will impact selections it makes later. This is desirable

44
00:04:27.359 --> 00:04:33.040
behavior because it means new tokens will make sense in the context of the tokens the LLM has

45
00:04:33.040 --> 00:04:40.239
already chosen. That said, it also means that once an LLM randomly chooses one direction to take a

46
00:04:40.239 --> 00:04:48.160
completion, it will follow that path where it leads. For example, after choosing shining, the LLM might

47
00:04:48.160 --> 00:04:56.799
choose in, the, and sky, since those words all make sense in the context of the words chosen before them.

48
00:04:56.799 --> 00:05:04.079
If that initial generated token had been warming, however, the LLM might then choose our

49
00:05:04.079 --> 00:05:12.000
faces, as that makes more sense given the direction the LLM took the completion. This behavior is

50
00:05:12.000 --> 00:05:20.799
called autoregressive, meaning self-influencing. Thanks to the randomness and autoregressive nature,

51
00:05:21.359 --> 00:05:27.440
running the same prompt multiple times through the same LLM will usually lead to different

52
00:05:27.440 --> 00:05:33.760
completions. An LLM is able to understand the meaning of a prompt and make reasonable predictions

53
00:05:33.760 --> 00:05:39.920
of words because it has been trained on large collections of text. The mathematical model that

54
00:05:39.920 --> 00:05:46.640
powers an LLM has billions of individual parameters or numerical weights. Before training, this model

55
00:05:46.720 --> 00:05:53.440
would just produce gibberish. During training, the LLM is shown incomplete pieces of text from the

56
00:05:53.440 --> 00:05:59.279
training data and tries to predict which word comes next. Based on the accuracy of these predictions,

57
00:05:59.279 --> 00:06:05.119
it will update its internal parameters. In this way, the model learns to produce both the factual

58
00:06:05.119 --> 00:06:11.119
information and the linguistic styles in the training data. Many large language models today

59
00:06:11.119 --> 00:06:16.880
are trained on trillions of tokens of text, largely taken from the open internet. The

60
00:06:16.880 --> 00:06:22.720
resulting models are able to generate text in a wide variety of styles and on wide variety of

61
00:06:22.720 --> 00:06:28.320
topics because examples of those styles and information of those topics was in its training

62
00:06:28.320 --> 00:06:34.160
data. Understanding how LLMs work and how they're trained also explains many of their behaviors.

63
00:06:34.959 --> 00:06:42.160
Let's start with hallucinations. All LLMs can do is generate probable sequences of words

64
00:06:42.160 --> 00:06:47.600
based on the patterns they learned in their training data. If you ask an LLM about your

65
00:06:47.600 --> 00:06:53.920
company's private internal data or today's news, the model almost certainly wasn't trained on that

66
00:06:53.920 --> 00:07:00.320
information and so isn't in a good position to respond. In these instances, an LLM will sometimes

67
00:07:00.320 --> 00:07:06.640
give responses that sound right but aren't actually true. Even though these behaviors are

68
00:07:06.640 --> 00:07:12.320
called hallucinations, it's important to remember the LLM isn't having a psychological episode

69
00:07:12.320 --> 00:07:19.519
or even really malfunctioning. LLMs are designed to generate probable text, not truthful text.

70
00:07:20.239 --> 00:07:27.119
Truth, as far as an LLM is concerned, is just that a sequence of words is probabilistically

71
00:07:27.119 --> 00:07:32.320
likely based on its training data. With high quality training data, our intuitive understanding

72
00:07:32.320 --> 00:07:38.000
of truth and an LLM's mathematical understanding of probable word sequences can be kept in alignment.

73
00:07:38.880 --> 00:07:44.640
The challenge then is to ensure the LLM has access to as much relevant information as possible.

74
00:07:45.279 --> 00:07:51.839
RAG solves this problem by taking advantage of how well LLMs are able to understand context.

75
00:07:52.640 --> 00:07:58.559
If your RAG system adds relevant information to the prompt, the LLM is able to understand and

76
00:07:58.559 --> 00:08:03.839
incorporate that information into its responses even though that information wasn't part of its

77
00:08:03.839 --> 00:08:10.640
training data. Typically you'd say this information grounds the LLM's responses. While you might just

78
00:08:10.640 --> 00:08:16.160
want to add as much relevant information to the prompt as you can find, in practice there's reasons

79
00:08:16.239 --> 00:08:23.200
you can't. First, longer prompts just take more computation to run. This is because before

80
00:08:23.200 --> 00:08:29.839
generating each new token, the model performs a computationally complex scan of every token

81
00:08:29.839 --> 00:08:36.239
already in the completion including the original prompt. Second, eventually you'll hit the limit

82
00:08:36.239 --> 00:08:43.119
of an LLM's context window, the longest sequence it can process at once. Some older models have

83
00:08:43.119 --> 00:08:49.520
context windows of only a few thousand tokens, while newer ones can process millions. This means

84
00:08:49.520 --> 00:08:54.640
that as the retriever adds more information to the augmented prompt, at first you'll just make

85
00:08:54.640 --> 00:08:59.760
your prompts more costly and eventually you'll entirely use up the model's context window.

86
00:09:00.719 --> 00:09:06.239
There are a wide array of LLM providers, but in this course you'll be using TogetherAI,

87
00:09:06.239 --> 00:09:12.239
which hosts many popular open source models. Using open source models makes it easier to look

88
00:09:12.239 --> 00:09:17.359
under the hood of large language models and explore many of the concepts you'll learn throughout this

89
00:09:17.359 --> 00:09:24.239
course. There's a lot more to learn about LLMs, but the main takeaway at this point is that an LLM's

90
00:09:24.239 --> 00:09:30.640
design allows it to incorporate information in the prompt into its response, even if that

91
00:09:30.640 --> 00:09:36.320
information wasn't included in the training data. So let's turn our attention to the retriever

92
00:09:36.320 --> 00:09:40.400
and see how it works to provide that information.

